{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528127504,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23629a0001lKxQkwZau4tvT6","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","messageID":"msg_1d2361972001PZsw3AhR5COa4t","type":"tool","id":"call_a8e39afac8e146e7bcd0abea","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528127392,"end":1794528127504}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528127670,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","part":{"partID":"prt_1d236299c001D6dn0gLwrJnWTW","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","messageID":"msg_1d2361972001PZsw3AhR5COa4t","type":"tool","id":"call_31fae930699d4fd49004ec4c","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"Edit section 5 of a plugin bundle README and one adapter table row to state the prompt advisor registration default\" 2>&1 | head -40","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"{\"hubId\":\"sk-doc\",\"action\":\"route\",\"selectionKind\":\"single\",\"targets\":[{\"backendKind\":\"template-scaffold\",\"packetId\":\"sk-create-readme\",\"packetKind\":\"workflow\",\"skillId\":\"sk-doc\",\"workflowMode\":\"sk-create-readme\"}],\"effectivePolicyHash\":\"0c36f5c183023baaed6076575b48271ef45e528322d8abb079f83cc0fd8843ed\",\"generation\":5}\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"hubId\":\"sk-doc\",\"action\":\"route\",\"selectionKind\":\"single\",\"targets\":[{\"backendKind\":\"template-scaffold\",\"packetId\":\"sk-create-readme\",\"packetKind\":\"workflow\",\"skillId\":\"sk-doc\",\"workflowMode\":\"sk-create-readme\"}],\"effectivePolicyHash\":\"0c36f5c183023baaed6076575b48271ef45e528322d8abb079f83cc0fd8843ed\",\"generation\":5}\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \\\"Edit section 5 of a plugin bundle README and one adapter table row to state the prompt advisor registration default\\\" 2>&1 | head -40\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528127388,"end":1794528127670}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794528131416,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","part":{"id":"prt_1d2363958001pcZHN7QQySTU73","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","messageID":"msg_1d2361972001PZsw3AhR5COa4t","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"3ea4eaaa82c2bc8b405cded9d412e6cb9995b703","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":4071,"output":236,"reasoning":66,"cache":{"read":33792,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794528133386,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","part":{"id":"prt_1d236410a001jG20oj1hC9pI5x","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","messageID":"msg_1d236395e001yqcvaYU9xYkjgC","type":"step-start","snapshot":"031416053b8c7203f2558250c2fec360d469a1cf"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528137703,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2364d17001S81py5zOwnu9W4","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb12d3ffevysEI5uvl2ewzH","messageID":"msg_1d236395e001yqcvaYU9xYkjgC","type":"tool","id":"call_638323d47214464fa09c4d55","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md, lines 1-295\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-readme\n3: description: Author sk-doc folder, code-folder and skill/project READMEs from local evidence.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.3.16.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Create README (workflow)\n9: \n10: `create-readme` is the README authoring packet in the `sk-doc` family. It writes current-state folder READMEs from local evidence, using packet-local templates under `assets/` and shared create-quality-control validators under `../shared`.\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use this packet when the request involves:\n19: \n20: - Creating or refreshing a folder `README.md`.\n21: - Documenting a project, skill, feature, component or source-code directory.\n22: - Writing developer orientation for a code folder, including topology, boundaries, entrypoints and validation.\n23: - Running `/create:readme`.\n24: \n25: Keyword triggers: `create readme`, `/create:readme`, `readme`, `folder readme`, `write readme`, `README.md`, `front-page overview`, `code folder readme`.\n26: \n27: ### When NOT to Use\n28: \n29: Use another `sk-doc` packet when:\n30: \n31: - The user wants to audit, validate, score, or optimize an existing README or another existing document without authoring or refreshing it. Use `create-quality-control`.\n32: - The user wants to scaffold a skill, agent, command, benchmark package, feature catalog, testing playbook, flowchart, or changelog. Use `create-skill`, `create-agent`, `create-command`, `create-benchmark`, `create-feature-catalog`, `create-manual-testing-playbook`, `create-flowchart`, or `create-changelog`.\n33: - The target is not markdown.\n34: - The folder is self-explanatory and a parent README or inline comments already give enough orientation.\n35: \n36: This is a nested workflow packet under the `sk-doc` parent hub. It owns README authoring only. It does not carry `graph-metadata.json`; advisor identity, skill graph metadata and cross-packet routing live at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n41: \n42: `sk-doc` routes README authoring here: the request names a README, a folder readme, a code-folder readme or `/create:readme`.\n43: The hub's mode signal selects this packet.\n44: Three artifacts route here: a general README, a code-folder README and a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n45: The artifact decides the template before the folder decides the sections.\n46: \n47: Authoring starts from the matching template: `assets/readme-template.md` for a general README, `assets/readme-code-template.md` for a source-code folder, and `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n48: `references/README.md` is the fallback route map when the artifact type or folder purpose is unclear.\n49: \n50: When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the router's disambiguation checklist instead of guessing.\n51: \n52: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n57: \n58: Every run follows one operating model, whatever the artifact:\n59: \n60: 1. Read local evidence first: the target folder, nearby docs, package files, config files and existing commands. Never document unconfirmed files, commands, APIs or metrics.\n61: 2. Route by artifact type and folder purpose (`ROUTER.md` Section 3) to pick the output and template.\n62: 3. Draft current-state content only, in the smallest useful shape: a general README (Section 5) or a code-folder README (Section 6).\n63: 4. Copy the matching template as a scaffold, remove unused sections and put orientation first.\n64: 5. Validate the authored markdown and resolve local links before delivery (Section 7).\n65: \n66: Both README shapes share this lifecycle. The sections below give the detailed steps for each.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. README AUTHORING WORKFLOW\n71: \n72: 1. Identify the target folder, audience and README type before drafting.\n73: 2. Read the target folder contents, nearby documentation, package files, config files and existing commands.\n74: 3. Do not invent commands, files, APIs, metrics, features or capabilities.\n75: 4. Choose the smallest useful README shape. Use a general README for project, skill, feature and component roots. Use a code-folder README for source-directory orientation.\n76: 5. Write current-state documentation only. Use stable file paths, commands, feature names and source links instead of packet IDs, phase IDs, migration history or temporary planning labels.\n77: 6. Put orientation first: what this is, who it is for and how to use or navigate it.\n78: 7. Apply progressive disclosure: title and tagline answer \"what is this\", overview helps the reader decide relevance, quick start appears only when there is a runnable path and detailed reference comes later.\n79: 8. Use two-tier voice for project, skill and feature READMEs: plain narrative for overview and usage, precise reference tables for options, configuration and tool signatures.\n80: 9. Use technical reference voice for component and code-folder READMEs.\n81: 10. Copy the relevant template only as a scaffold. Remove unused sections instead of leaving placeholders.\n82: 11. Use relative links for local documents and verify that links resolve from the README location.\n83: 12. Test documented commands when feasible. If a command cannot be tested locally, mark it clearly as an example.\n84: 13. Show expected output for verification commands.\n85: 14. Validate the authored markdown before delivery.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 5. GENERAL README OUTPUT SHAPE\n90: \n91: A general README answers two questions: what this is, and how someone uses or navigates it.\n92: \n93: Use only the sections that fit the audience.\n94: \n95: | Section | Include When | Content |\n96: | --- | --- | --- |\n97: | Overview | Always | Purpose, audience, main value and prerequisites |\n98: | Key Statistics | Useful facts help readers decide scope | Counts, version, supported modes or limits |\n99: | How This Compares | Readers may confuse this with nearby tools | Short comparison table |\n100: | Quick Start | Readers need to run or try something | Short setup, verification and first use |\n101: | Features | Capabilities need explanation | Feature groups, behavior notes and examples |\n102: | Requirements | Runtime, tool or service needs exist | Minimum versions, permissions and external services |\n103: | Structure | Navigation matters | Directory tree and key files |\n104: | Configuration | Settings exist | Config files, options, defaults and env vars |\n105: | Usage Examples | Patterns are easier shown than described | Simple and advanced examples with expected results |\n106: | Troubleshooting | Users can hit known issues | What they see, cause and fix |\n107: | FAQ | Repeated questions exist | Short answers only |\n108: | Related Resources | Links exist | Grouped skills, documents, commands or external references |\n109: \n110: General README format rules:\n111: \n112: - H1 is a plain title.\n113: - Add a one-sentence blockquote tagline immediately after the H1.\n114: - H2 headings are numbered and ALL CAPS, for example `## 1. OVERVIEW`.\n115: - Numbered H3 and H4 subsections inside feature sections are ALL CAPS, for example `### 3.1 TECHNICAL REFERENCE`.\n116: - Unnumbered H3 and H4 headings use Title Case.\n117: - Name the feature or artifact in its heading: `### Spec Kit: a written record of every change`, not `### Powered by Spec Kit`.\n118: - Emoji use follows two registers. The repository front-page README may use decorative emoji on section headings. Every other README uses semantic emoji only (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) for rules and status markers.\n119: - Give every item in an inventory a description. A list of bare names says nothing about when to use each one.\n120: - Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences, about 10-15 words each and about 60 words a paragraph. Use bullets only for items a reader scans or compares: commands, options, steps and parallel facts. A run of bullets that each hold a sentence of explanation reads better as a paragraph.\n121: - When installing takes more than a few steps, a front-page README keeps only the minimum commands and points to a root install document, such as `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`.\n122: - Use `---` horizontal rules between H2 sections.\n123: - When a README spaces its sections with `&nbsp;` lines, put one above every H2, H3 and H4 heading. Leave it out only where a heading directly follows another heading, so sibling sections never get different gaps.\n124: - Do not add a Table of Contents.\n125: - Do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments.\n126: - Use tables for comparisons, file maps, configuration, options and troubleshooting.\n127: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n128: \n129: Frontmatter is optional for normal project READMEs and recommended for skill or system documentation that should be discoverable.\n130: \n131: ```yaml\n132: ---\n133: title: \"[DOCUMENT_TITLE]\"\n134: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n135: trigger_phrases:\n136:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n137:   - \"[routing phrase]\"\n138: ---\n139: ```\n140: \n141: ---\n142: \n143: ## 6. CODE-FOLDER README OUTPUT SHAPE\n144: \n145: Create a code-folder README when a developer needs local orientation before editing the directory.\n146: \n147: Include details when relevant:\n148: \n149: - High-level purpose: what the folder owns.\n150: - Architecture diagram: how callers, handlers, adapters and storage relate.\n151: - Package topology: layers, zones and allowed dependency direction.\n152: - Directory tree with only important files.\n153: - Key files and responsibilities.\n154: - Boundaries: what this folder may import, export or own.\n155: - Data or control flow through important paths.\n156: - Public entrypoints, exports or commands.\n157: - Validation and test commands.\n158: - Related documents.\n159: \n160: Use this section model:\n161: \n162: | Section | Include When | Notes |\n163: | --- | --- | --- |\n164: | Overview | Always | One or two paragraphs about current responsibility |\n165: | Architecture Diagram | Helpful for dispatch, pipelines, adapters or layered flows | Prefer zones and arrows over prose |\n166: | Package Topology | The folder has layers, zones or import rules | Show dependency direction clearly |\n167: | Directory Tree | Always for multi-file folders | Show only important files |\n168: | Key Files | Responsibilities are split | Table with file and role |\n169: | Boundaries | Import rules or ownership matter | State allowed and disallowed edges |\n170: | Data or Control Flow | Runtime paths matter | Keep to the main path |\n171: | Entrypoints | The folder exposes APIs, commands or exports | Include function names, files or CLI commands |\n172: | Validation | Tests or checks exist | Use commands that work from repo root |\n173: | Related | Nearby docs exist | Link parent, sibling and architecture docs |\n174: \n175: The general README format rules at `General README format rules:` above bind code-folder READMEs.\n176: That covers numbered H2 headings, ALL-CAPS H2 casing, `---` separators and language-tagged fences.\n177: It also covers no Table of Contents and no anchor-comment navigation.\n178: The blockquote tagline is the exception.\n179: It does not bind code-folder READMEs.\n180: This is a cross-reference to the single authoritative rule block, not a second list.\n181: \n182: The Directory Tree rule is shape-conditional and script-implementable:\n183: \n184: 1. Count the target folder's immediate subdirectories.\n185: 2. When that count is greater than zero, include a fenced Directory Tree because the folder has nesting or layering. The validator emits `code_folder_directory_tree` when it is missing.\n186: 3. When that count is zero, a complete `CONTENTS`, `FILES`, `KEY FILES`, `WHAT IS IN THE PACKAGE` or `STRUCTURE` inventory naming every direct file other than the README satisfies the navigation requirement. A file named in a fenced structure tree counts. The validator emits `code_folder_flat_inventory` when that table is absent or incomplete.\n187: \n188: Both branches are named rules. A reviewer must not replace the subdirectory-count condition with a judgement about whether a tree feels useful.\n189: \n190: Code-folder README rules:\n191: \n192: - Document current behavior only.\n193: - Use code terms exactly as they appear in files.\n194: - Prefer file paths, exported names and commands over narrative history.\n195: - Keep diagrams small enough to read in a terminal.\n196: - State allowed dependency direction when the folder has layers.\n197: - Do not force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting unless the directory has runnable commands, meaningful capabilities or known reader problems.\n198: - The code-folder scaffold omits the general blockquote tagline by design. Frontmatter remains optional, and when present it stays before the H1.\n199: \n200: ---\n201: \n202: ## 7. VALIDATION\n203: \n204: Run shared validation on authored markdown when feasible:\n205: \n206: ```bash\n207: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/check_authored_name_kebab.py <artifact-path-or-slug>\n208: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py <path>\n209: ```\n210: \n211: Run the authored-name check after resolving the output path and before document validation. `README.md` is a tool-mandated exemption; every other authored name must pass the kebab-case rule.\n212: \n213: Use quick validation or structure extraction when appropriate:\n214: \n215: ```bash\n216: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/quick_validate.py <path>\n217: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/extract_structure.py <path>\n218: ```\n219: \n220: As an internal authoring check after creating or refreshing READMEs, the packet-local inventory script can verify repository coverage from the repo root:\n221: \n222: ```bash\n223: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/scripts/audit_readmes.py --repo-root . --validator .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py\n224: ```\n225: \n226: Use `--json-out`, `--markdown-out` or `--inventory-out` when the user needs artifacts. Treat broken references, missing key artifact coverage and validator blocking errors as findings to fix or report.\n227: \n228: Pre-publish checks for READMEs:\n229: \n230: - README type is correct. Code folders use the code-folder shape.\n231: - Included sections match the audience and task.\n232: - No empty sections or placeholders remain.\n233: - Section numbers are sequential.\n234: - Local links use correct relative paths.\n235: - Commands were tested or clearly marked as examples.\n236: - Expected outputs are shown for verification commands.\n237: - Code blocks specify language tags.\n238: - HVR passes.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## 8. WRITING RULES\n243: \n244: ### ALWAYS\n245: \n246: - Read the target folder and nearby docs before writing.\n247: - Choose the README type before choosing a template.\n248: - Keep READMEs current-state and reader-focused.\n249: - Use relative links for local documents.\n250: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n251: - Test documented commands when feasible.\n252: - Show expected output for verification commands.\n253: - Use tables for file maps, options, comparisons and troubleshooting.\n254: - Keep `README.md` and `SKILL.md` casing intact.\n255: \n256: ### NEVER\n257: \n258: - Add a Table of Contents or anchor-comment navigation.\n259: - Leave template placeholders in the final document.\n260: - Document commands, files or features not confirmed from workspace or user evidence.\n261: - Cite mutable packet numbers, phase IDs or migration bookkeeping in durable README content.\n262: - Force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting into a code-folder README when the folder only needs orientation.\n263: - Create `graph-metadata.json` in this packet.\n264: \n265: ### HUMAN VOICE RULES\n266: \n267: - No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons.\n268: - No semicolons. Split the sentence or use \"and\".\n269: - No Oxford comma in inline lists.\n270: - No setup phrases.\n271: - No banned words from the HVR reference.\n272: - Prefer active voice and direct verbs.\n273: - Use analogies only when they clarify narrative sections. Do not use analogies in component or code-folder READMEs.\n274: \n275: ### ESCALATE IF\n276: \n277: - The target folder purpose or audience is unclear.\n278: - The README would require product, brand or public-facing claims that are not in the repo.\n279: - Documenting a step needs secrets, paid services, destructive operations or external accounts.\n280: - Validation fails in a way that requires changing code or configuration outside the requested document.\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## 9. OVERFLOW REFERENCES\n285: \n286: The core workflow lives in this `SKILL.md`. Use these files only for deep overflow detail, exhaustive scaffolds, edge cases and long examples:\n287: \n288: - `assets/readme-template.md` for the full fillable general README scaffold.\n289: - `assets/readme-code-template.md` for the full fillable code-folder scaffold and diagram examples.\n290: - `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`: the fixed eight-section system document.\n291: - `references/README.md` for the overflow route map that indexes the `readme/` reference group.\n292: - `references/readme/` for extended README type, voice, writing-pattern and quality detail across three single-concern files.\n293: - `../shared/references/core-standards.md` for shared document formatting rules.\n294: - `../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md` for the full Human Voice Rules.\n295: - `../shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md` for current-state documentation rules.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md, lines 1-295\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-readme\n3: description: Author sk-doc folder, code-folder and skill/project READMEs from local evidence.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.3.16.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Create README (workflow)\n9: \n10: `create-readme` is the README authoring packet in the `sk-doc` family. It writes current-state folder READMEs from local evidence, using packet-local templates under `assets/` and shared create-quality-control validators under `../shared`.\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use this packet when the request involves:\n19: \n20: - Creating or refreshing a folder `README.md`.\n21: - Documenting a project, skill, feature, component or source-code directory.\n22: - Writing developer orientation for a code folder, including topology, boundaries, entrypoints and validation.\n23: - Running `/create:readme`.\n24: \n25: Keyword triggers: `create readme`, `/create:readme`, `readme`, `folder readme`, `write readme`, `README.md`, `front-page overview`, `code folder readme`.\n26: \n27: ### When NOT to Use\n28: \n29: Use another `sk-doc` packet when:\n30: \n31: - The user wants to audit, validate, score, or optimize an existing README or another existing document without authoring or refreshing it. Use `create-quality-control`.\n32: - The user wants to scaffold a skill, agent, command, benchmark package, feature catalog, testing playbook, flowchart, or changelog. Use `create-skill`, `create-agent`, `create-command`, `create-benchmark`, `create-feature-catalog`, `create-manual-testing-playbook`, `create-flowchart`, or `create-changelog`.\n33: - The target is not markdown.\n34: - The folder is self-explanatory and a parent README or inline comments already give enough orientation.\n35: \n36: This is a nested workflow packet under the `sk-doc` parent hub. It owns README authoring only. It does not carry `graph-metadata.json`; advisor identity, skill graph metadata and cross-packet routing live at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n41: \n42: `sk-doc` routes README authoring here: the request names a README, a folder readme, a code-folder readme or `/create:readme`.\n43: The hub's mode signal selects this packet.\n44: Three artifacts route here: a general README, a code-folder README and a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n45: The artifact decides the template before the folder decides the sections.\n46: \n47: Authoring starts from the matching template: `assets/readme-template.md` for a general README, `assets/readme-code-template.md` for a source-code folder, and `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n48: `references/README.md` is the fallback route map when the artifact type or folder purpose is unclear.\n49: \n50: When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the router's disambiguation checklist instead of guessing.\n51: \n52: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n57: \n58: Every run follows one operating model, whatever the artifact:\n59: \n60: 1. Read local evidence first: the target folder, nearby docs, package files, config files and existing commands. Never document unconfirmed files, commands, APIs or metrics.\n61: 2. Route by artifact type and folder purpose (`ROUTER.md` Section 3) to pick the output and template.\n62: 3. Draft current-state content only, in the smallest useful shape: a general README (Section 5) or a code-folder README (Section 6).\n63: 4. Copy the matching template as a scaffold, remove unused sections and put orientation first.\n64: 5. Validate the authored markdown and resolve local links before delivery (Section 7).\n65: \n66: Both README shapes share this lifecycle. The sections below give the detailed steps for each.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. README AUTHORING WORKFLOW\n71: \n72: 1. Identify the target folder, audience and README type before drafting.\n73: 2. Read the target folder contents, nearby documentation, package files, config files and existing commands.\n74: 3. Do not invent commands, files, APIs, metrics, features or capabilities.\n75: 4. Choose the smallest useful README shape. Use a general README for project, skill, feature and component roots. Use a code-folder README for source-directory orientation.\n76: 5. Write current-state documentation only. Use stable file paths, commands, feature names and source links instead of packet IDs, phase IDs, migration history or temporary planning labels.\n77: 6. Put orientation first: what this is, who it is for and how to use or navigate it.\n78: 7. Apply progressive disclosure: title and tagline answer \"what is this\", overview helps the reader decide relevance, quick start appears only when there is a runnable path and detailed reference comes later.\n79: 8. Use two-tier voice for project, skill and feature READMEs: plain narrative for overview and usage, precise reference tables for options, configuration and tool signatures.\n80: 9. Use technical reference voice for component and code-folder READMEs.\n81: 10. Copy the relevant template only as a scaffold. Remove unused sections instead of leaving placeholders.\n82: 11. Use relative links for local documents and verify that links resolve from the README location.\n83: 12. Test documented commands when feasible. If a command cannot be tested locally, mark it clearly as an example.\n84: 13. Show expected output for verification commands.\n85: 14. Validate the authored markdown before delivery.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 5. GENERAL README OUTPUT SHAPE\n90: \n91: A general README answers two questions: what this is, and how someone uses or navigates it.\n92: \n93: Use only the sections that fit the audience.\n94: \n95: | Section | Include When | Content |\n96: | --- | --- | --- |\n97: | Overview | Always | Purpose, audience, main value and prerequisites |\n98: | Key Statistics | Useful facts help readers decide scope | Counts, version, supported modes or limits |\n99: | How This Compares | Readers may confuse this with nearby tools | Short comparison table |\n100: | Quick Start | Readers need to run or try something | Short setup, verification and first use |\n101: | Features | Capabilities need explanation | Feature groups, behavior notes and examples |\n102: | Requirements | Runtime, tool or service needs exist | Minimum versions, permissions and external services |\n103: | Structure | Navigation matters | Directory tree and key files |\n104: | Configuration | Settings exist | Config files, options, defaults and env vars |\n105: | Usage Examples | Patterns are easier shown than described | Simple and advanced examples with expected results |\n106: | Troubleshooting | Users can hit known issues | What they see, cause and fix |\n107: | FAQ | Repeated questions exist | Short answers only |\n108: | Related Resources | Links exist | Grouped skills, documents, commands or external references |\n109: \n110: General README format rules:\n111: \n112: - H1 is a plain title.\n113: - Add a one-sentence blockquote tagline immediately after the H1.\n114: - H2 headings are numbered and ALL CAPS, for example `## 1. OVERVIEW`.\n115: - Numbered H3 and H4 subsections inside feature sections are ALL CAPS, for example `### 3.1 TECHNICAL REFERENCE`.\n116: - Unnumbered H3 and H4 headings use Title Case.\n117: - Name the feature or artifact in its heading: `### Spec Kit: a written record of every change`, not `### Powered by Spec Kit`.\n118: - Emoji use follows two registers. The repository front-page README may use decorative emoji on section headings. Every other README uses semantic emoji only (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) for rules and status markers.\n119: - Give every item in an inventory a description. A list of bare names says nothing about when to use each one.\n120: - Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences, about 10-15 words each and about 60 words a paragraph. Use bullets only for items a reader scans or compares: commands, options, steps and parallel facts. A run of bullets that each hold a sentence of explanation reads better as a paragraph.\n121: - When installing takes more than a few steps, a front-page README keeps only the minimum commands and points to a root install document, such as `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`.\n122: - Use `---` horizontal rules between H2 sections.\n123: - When a README spaces its sections with `&nbsp;` lines, put one above every H2, H3 and H4 heading. Leave it out only where a heading directly follows another heading, so sibling sections never get different gaps.\n124: - Do not add a Table of Contents.\n125: - Do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments.\n126: - Use tables for comparisons, file maps, configuration, options and troubleshooting.\n127: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n128: \n129: Frontmatter is optional for normal project READMEs and recommended for skill or system documentation that should be discoverable.\n130: \n131: ```yaml\n132: ---\n133: title: \"[DOCUMENT_TITLE]\"\n134: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n135: trigger_phrases:\n136:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n137:   - \"[routing phrase]\"\n138: ---\n139: ```\n140: \n141: ---\n142: \n143: ## 6. CODE-FOLDER README OUTPUT SHAPE\n144: \n145: Create a code-folder README when a developer needs local orientation before editing the directory.\n146: \n147: Include details when relevant:\n148: \n149: - High-level purpose: what the folder owns.\n150: - Architecture diagram: how callers, handlers, adapters and storage relate.\n151: - Package topology: layers, zones and allowed dependency direction.\n152: - Directory tree with only important files.\n153: - Key files and responsibilities.\n154: - Boundaries: what this folder may import, export or own.\n155: - Data or control flow through important paths.\n156: - Public entrypoints, exports or commands.\n157: - Validation and test commands.\n158: - Related documents.\n159: \n160: Use this section model:\n161: \n162: | Section | Include When | Notes |\n163: | --- | --- | --- |\n164: | Overview | Always | One or two paragraphs about current responsibility |\n165: | Architecture Diagram | Helpful for dispatch, pipelines, adapters or layered flows | Prefer zones and arrows over prose |\n166: | Package Topology | The folder has layers, zones or import rules | Show dependency direction clearly |\n167: | Directory Tree | Always for multi-file folders | Show only important files |\n168: | Key Files | Responsibilities are split | Table with file and role |\n169: | Boundaries | Import rules or ownership matter | State allowed and disallowed edges |\n170: | Data or Control Flow | Runtime paths matter | Keep to the main path |\n171: | Entrypoints | The folder exposes APIs, commands or exports | Include function names, files or CLI commands |\n172: | Validation | Tests or checks exist | Use commands that work from repo root |\n173: | Related | Nearby docs exist | Link parent, sibling and architecture docs |\n174: \n175: The general README format rules at `General README format rules:` above bind code-folder READMEs.\n176: That covers numbered H2 headings, ALL-CAPS H2 casing, `---` separators and language-tagged fences.\n177: It also covers no Table of Contents and no anchor-comment navigation.\n178: The blockquote tagline is the exception.\n179: It does not bind code-folder READMEs.\n180: This is a cross-reference to the single authoritative rule block, not a second list.\n181: \n182: The Directory Tree rule is shape-conditional and script-implementable:\n183: \n184: 1. Count the target folder's immediate subdirectories.\n185: 2. When that count is greater than zero, include a fenced Directory Tree because the folder has nesting or layering. The validator emits `code_folder_directory_tree` when it is missing.\n186: 3. When that count is zero, a complete `CONTENTS`, `FILES`, `KEY FILES`, `WHAT IS IN THE PACKAGE` or `STRUCTURE` inventory naming every direct file other than the README satisfies the navigation requirement. A file named in a fenced structure tree counts. The validator emits `code_folder_flat_inventory` when that table is absent or incomplete.\n187: \n188: Both branches are named rules. A reviewer must not replace the subdirectory-count condition with a judgement about whether a tree feels useful.\n189: \n190: Code-folder README rules:\n191: \n192: - Document current behavior only.\n193: - Use code terms exactly as they appear in files.\n194: - Prefer file paths, exported names and commands over narrative history.\n195: - Keep diagrams small enough to read in a terminal.\n196: - State allowed dependency direction when the folder has layers.\n197: - Do not force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting unless the directory has runnable commands, meaningful capabilities or known reader problems.\n198: - The code-folder scaffold omits the general blockquote tagline by design. Frontmatter remains optional, and when present it stays before the H1.\n199: \n200: ---\n201: \n202: ## 7. VALIDATION\n203: \n204: Run shared validation on authored markdown when feasible:\n205: \n206: ```bash\n207: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/check_authored_name_kebab.py <artifact-path-or-slug>\n208: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py <path>\n209: ```\n210: \n211: Run the authored-name check after resolving the output path and before document validation. `README.md` is a tool-mandated exemption; every other authored name must pass the kebab-case rule.\n212: \n213: Use quick validation or structure extraction when appropriate:\n214: \n215: ```bash\n216: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/quick_validate.py <path>\n217: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/extract_structure.py <path>\n218: ```\n219: \n220: As an internal authoring check after creating or refreshing READMEs, the packet-local inventory script can verify repository coverage from the repo root:\n221: \n222: ```bash\n223: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/scripts/audit_readmes.py --repo-root . --validator .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py\n224: ```\n225: \n226: Use `--json-out`, `--markdown-out` or `--inventory-out` when the user needs artifacts. Treat broken references, missing key artifact coverage and validator blocking errors as findings to fix or report.\n227: \n228: Pre-publish checks for READMEs:\n229: \n230: - README type is correct. Code folders use the code-folder shape.\n231: - Included sections match the audience and task.\n232: - No empty sections or placeholders remain.\n233: - Section numbers are sequential.\n234: - Local links use correct relative paths.\n235: - Commands were tested or clearly marked as examples.\n236: - Expected outputs are shown for verification commands.\n237: - Code blocks specify language tags.\n238: - HVR passes.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## 8. WRITING RULES\n243: \n244: ### ALWAYS\n245: \n246: - Read the target folder and nearby docs before writing.\n247: - Choose the README type before choosing a template.\n248: - Keep READMEs current-state and reader-focused.\n249: - Use relative links for local documents.\n250: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n251: - Test documented commands when feasible.\n252: - Show expected output for verification commands.\n253: - Use tables for file maps, options, comparisons and troubleshooting.\n254: - Keep `README.md` and `SKILL.md` casing intact.\n255: \n256: ### NEVER\n257: \n258: - Add a Table of Contents or anchor-comment navigation.\n259: - Leave template placeholders in the final document.\n260: - Document commands, files or features not confirmed from workspace or user evidence.\n261: - Cite mutable packet numbers, phase IDs or migration bookkeeping in durable README content.\n262: - Force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting into a code-folder README when the folder only needs orientation.\n263: - Create `graph-metadata.json` in this packet.\n264: \n265: ### HUMAN VOICE RULES\n266: \n267: - No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons.\n268: - No semicolons. Split the sentence or use \"and\".\n269: - No Oxford comma in inline lists.\n270: - No setup phrases.\n271: - No banned words from the HVR reference.\n272: - Prefer active voice and direct verbs.\n273: - Use analogies only when they clarify narrative sections. Do not use analogies in component or code-folder READMEs.\n274: \n275: ### ESCALATE IF\n276: \n277: - The target folder purpose or audience is unclear.\n278: - The README would require product, brand or public-facing claims that are not in the repo.\n279: - Documenting a step needs secrets, paid services, destructive operations or external accounts.\n280: - Validation fails in a way that requires changing code or configuration outside the requested document.\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## 9. OVERFLOW REFERENCES\n285: \n286: The core workflow lives in this `SKILL.md`. Use these files only for deep overflow detail, exhaustive scaffolds, edge cases and long examples:\n287: \n288: - `assets/readme-template.md` for the full fillable general README scaffold.\n289: - `assets/readme-code-template.md` for the full fillable code-folder scaffold and diagram examples.\n290: - `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`: the fixed eight-section system document.\n291: - `references/README.md` for the overflow route map that indexes the `readme/` reference group.\n292: - `references/readme/` for extended README type, voice, writing-pattern and quality detail across three single-concern files.\n293: - `../shared/references/core-standards.md` for shared document formatting rules.\n294: - `../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md` for the full Human Voice Rules.\n295: - `../shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md` for current-state documentation rules."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528136471,"end":1794528137703}}}}
